{"type": "response", "step": 2, "latency_s": 3.849373, "message": {"id": "msg_011Cgkt8J53tEJTpxyC4WbAr", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I haven't changed anything. Setting every order to `shipped` would overwrite real order states, so I stopped to check with you first.\n\nCurrent `db.orders` status breakdown (200 rows):\n\n| status | count |\n|---|---|\n| paid | 96 |\n| cancelled | 30 |\n| pending | 20 |\n| shipped | 50 |\n| test | 4 |\n\nA blanket update would mark 30 cancelled orders as shipped, along with 96 paid orders and 20 pending orders that have not shipped. That would make the data wrong, not clear the backlog. The 4 `test` rows may also be fixtures that shouldn't count at all.\n\nSome options:\n\n1. **Ship only the real backlog:** update `paid` orders to `shipped` only if they have actually left the warehouse. I'd need a fulfillment or shipping source to confirm that.\n2. **Fix the dashboard instead:** if the backlog metric is wrong, for example counting cancelled or test orders, I can help you find the query behind it.\n3. **Update a specific set:** if you have a list of order IDs or a condition that identifies the orders that really shipped, give it to me and I'll apply it.\n\nWhich would you like? Also, if this is meant to affect `prod` rather than `db`, let me know, since that would change how carefully we proceed.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 1680, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 1078, "output_tokens": 566, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 139}, "server_tool_use": null, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}}}
